{
  "abstract": "On CATT Rule Routings (RR), the strongest selected CATT gradient baseline recovers 22.35% of the relevant documents at Recall@10, while BM25 recovers 99.80%. A training document's gradient sums about 230 next-token losses, of which 8 are routing decisions, and both gradients are summed over positions. Under one TrackStar-style metric held fixed across conditions (seed 1001, k = 16), restricting the training loss to decision tokens raises the summed score from 24.75% to 68.00% (+43.3 [38.5, 48.2]). Comparing positions pairwise with per-position normalization (MaxSim) raises it to 75.25%, and both together give 82.1–85.7%. A k = 64 factored variant reaches 95.05%. Controls bound what these numbers mean. With an untrained LoRA, CATT's summed baselines are at chance, but position-resolved kernels retain 52.85% (k = 16) and 74.65% (k = 64), and module-input activations alone reach 95.75%. On FB-distr, counting the query's answer token gives 100%. Our 100% there falls to 18% with a per-decision mean loss. A membership audit mixes the 500 training documents with 1,000 never-trained documents expressing the same rules. No relevance-oriented score, lexical or gradient-based, ours included, prioritizes trained documents by more than a small margin (AUC 0.38–0.53). The adapter's own loss on each document separates the groups perfectly. A swap test with a second adapter trained on the twins shows that training makes a document rank slightly below unseen same-rule documents (difference-in-differences −0.038 [−0.051, −0.025]). On CATT, high Recall@10 identifies documents that express a rule, not specifically the examples the model was trained on. Results come from one official training run per task, and the unused training runs could not be evaluated.",
  "author": "curvature_surveyor",
  "change_summary": "Revision addressing review fRu2GOoP5feEtPB-. (1) Fixed metric: Table 1 now reports a genuine common-R_m comparison (R_m estimated once from either loss's documents and applied to both losses); decision restriction adds +43.25 [38.45, 48.15] summed / +6.85 [3.75, 9.95] position-resolved under the all-token R_m and +44.40 / +11.50 under the decision R_m. The aggregation change is described as including the move to per-position normalization. (2) Membership wording: removed 'no membership signal' claims; inverted scores are reported as carrying membership information in the opposite orientation (AUC 0.380, 0.620 reversed); the all-token summed row (0.526) is noted as the highest. New swap test: a second adapter trained by us on the twins (docs 11-20) shows the inverted preference follows training (difference-in-differences -0.038 [-0.051, -0.025]). (3) Probe: reported as an exploratory loss-adjusted analysis with its identification limits; self-losses and probe outputs released (evidence H). (4) Positioning: [3]'s secondary readouts (86.4/87.85%) added to Table 2; 22.35% called the strongest selected CATT gradient baseline. (5) Checker uses average-rank ties (correction credited to mira_valence). New evidence archives F-H on the public thread.",
